   "subject": "Generic linear mixed effects model",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">So now I&#39;m trying to increase the complexity of my model. I&#39;d like to add a random effect. The basic model structure might look in very simple form like:<div><br></div><div><div>Y ~ X0 + X1 + X2 + (1|random)</div><div><br></div><div>with X0 being the full model intercept. Building on my last model, I have come up with this code based on an example <a href=\"http://arxiv.org/pdf/1506.06201.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://arxiv.org/pdf/1506.06201.pdf&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792462792696000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0EMf0_b0FSQLfZF_0GJyRm\">here</a>:<div><br></div><div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><p style=\"font-size:11px;line-height:normal;font-family:Menlo\"><span style=\"color:#000\">diet_code </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"font-variant-ligatures:no-common-ligatures;color:#d12f1b\"><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;&quot;&quot;</span></span></p><p style=\"font-size:11px;line-height:normal;font-family:Menlo;color:rgb(209,47,27)\"><span style=\"color:#080\">    data {</span></p><p style=\"font-size:11px;line-height:normal;font-family:Menlo;color:rgb(209,47,27)\"><span style=\"color:#080\">      int&lt;lower=0&gt; N; //rows of data</span></p><p style=\"font-size:11px;line-height:normal;font-family:Menlo;color:rgb(209,47,27)\"><span style=\"color:#080\">      int&lt;lower=0&gt; K; // number of covariates</span></p><p style=\"font-size:11px;line-height:normal;font-family:Menlo;color:rgb(209,47,27)\"><span style=\"color:#080\">      int&lt;lower=0&gt; J; // number of groups (random effects)</span></p><p style=\"font-size:11px;line-height:normal;font-family:Menlo;color:rgb(209,47,27)\"><span style=\"color:#080\">      matrix[N,K] x; // predictor matrix</span></p><p style=\"font-size:11px;line-height:normal;font-family:Menlo;color:rgb(209,47,27)\"><span style=\"color:#080\">      real y[N]; // response</span></p><p style=\"font-size:11px;line-height:normal;font-family:Menlo;color:rgb(209,47,27)\"><span style=\"color:#080\">      int&lt;lower=0, upper=J-1&gt; random[N]; //random effect (my data starts at </span><span style=\"color:#080\">0)</span></p><p style=\"font-size:11px;line-height:normal;font-family:Menlo;color:rgb(209,47,27)\"><span style=\"color:#080\">    }</span></p><p style=\"font-size:11px;line-height:normal;font-family:Menlo;color:rgb(209,47,27)\"><span style=\"color:#080\">    parameters {</span></p><p style=\"font-size:11px;line-height:normal;font-family:Menlo;color:rgb(209,47,27)\"><span style=\"color:#080\">      vector[K] beta; //intercept and slope parameters</span></p><p style=\"font-size:11px;line-height:normal;font-family:Menlo;color:rgb(209,47,27)\"><span style=\"color:#080\">      real&lt;lower=0, upper=10&gt; sigma; // intercept SD</span></p><p style=\"font-size:11px;line-height:normal;font-family:Menlo;color:rgb(209,47,27)\"><span style=\"color:#080\">      vector[J] u; // random intercepts</span></p><p style=\"font-size:11px;line-height:normal;font-family:Menlo;color:rgb(209,47,27)\"><span style=\"color:#080\">      real&lt;lower=0, upper=10&gt; sigma_u; // random intercepts SD</span></p><p style=\"font-size:11px;line-height:normal;font-family:Menlo;color:rgb(209,47,27)\"><span style=\"color:#080\">    }</span></p><p style=\"font-size:11px;line-height:normal;font-family:Menlo;color:rgb(209,47,27)\"><span style=\"color:#080\">    model {</span></p><p style=\"font-size:11px;line-height:normal;font-family:Menlo;color:rgb(209,47,27)\"><span style=\"color:#080\">      // local variables</span></p><p style=\"font-size:11px;line-height:normal;font-family:Menlo;color:rgb(209,47,27)\"><span style=\"color:#080\">      vector[N] mu;</span></p><p style=\"font-size:11px;line-height:normal;font-family:Menlo;color:rgb(209,47,27)\"><span style=\"color:#080\">      // priors</span></p><p style=\"font-size:11px;line-height:normal;font-family:Menlo;color:rgb(209,47,27)\"><span style=\"color:#080\">      u ~ normal(0,sigma_u);</span></p><p style=\"font-size:11px;line-height:normal;font-family:Menlo;color:rgb(209,47,27)\"><span style=\"color:#080\">      beta ~ normal(0,10);</span></p><p style=\"font-size:11px;line-height:normal;font-family:Menlo;color:rgb(209,47,27)\"><span style=\"color:#080\">      // data model</span></p><p style=\"font-size:11px;line-height:normal;font-family:Menlo;color:rgb(209,47,27)\"><span style=\"color:#080\">      mu[N] &lt;- x[N] * beta + u[random[N]];</span></p><p style=\"font-size:11px;line-height:normal;font-family:Menlo;color:rgb(209,47,27)\"><span style=\"color:#080\">      y ~ normal(mu, sigma);</span></p><p style=\"font-size:11px;line-height:normal;font-family:Menlo;color:rgb(209,47,27)\"><span style=\"color:#080\">    }</span></p><p style=\"font-size:11px;line-height:normal;font-family:Menlo;color:rgb(209,47,27)\"><span style=\"color:#080\">    &quot;&quot;&quot;</span></p></div></code></div><br>Surprisingly to me, the code actually compiles. But I get an error:</div><div><br></div><div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><font color=\"#660066\"><div>Informational Message: The current Metropolis proposal is about to be rejected because of the following issue:</div><div>Exception thrown at line 24: stan::math::lognormal_log: Location parameter[1] is nan, but must be finite!</div><div>If this warning occurs sporadically, such as for highly constrained variable types like covariance matrices, then the sampler is fine,</div><div>but if this warning occurs often then your model may be either severely ill-conditioned or misspecified.</div><div><br></div><div>Rejecting initial value:</div><div>  Error evaluating the log probability at the initial value.</div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div>Initialization between (-2, 2) failed after 100 attempts. </div><div> Try specifying initial values, reducing ranges of constrained values, or reparameterizing the model.</div></font></div></code></div><br>Using lme in R, I know this model has a solution. This seems it could very easily be something to do with my constraints or priors. What is going on with this model? Am I even in the ballpark?</div><div><br></div></div></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
