     3,
     "&gt; So now I&#39;m trying to increase the complexity of my model. I&#39;d like to add a random effect. The basic model structure might look in very simple form like:\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; Y ~ X0 + X1 + X2 + (1|random)\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; with X0 being the full model intercept. Building on my last model, I have come up with this code based on an example here:\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; diet_code = &quot;&quot;&quot;\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt;    data {\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt;      int&lt;lower=0&gt; N; //rows of data\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt;      int&lt;lower=0&gt; K; // number of covariates\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt;      int&lt;lower=0&gt; J; // number of groups (random effects)\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt;      matrix[N,K] x; // predictor matrix\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt;      real y[N]; // response\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt;      int&lt;lower=0, upper=J-1&gt; random[N]; //random effect (my data starts at 0)\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt;    }\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt;    parameters {\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt;      vector[K] beta; //intercept and slope parameters\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt;      real&lt;lower=0, upper=10&gt; sigma; // intercept SD\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt;      vector[J] u; // random intercepts\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt;      real&lt;lower=0, upper=10&gt; sigma_u; // random intercepts SD\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt;    }\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt;    model {\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt;      // local variables\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt;      vector[N] mu;\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt;      // priors\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt;      u ~ normal(0,sigma_u);\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt;      beta ~ normal(0,10);\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt;      // data model\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt;      mu[N] &lt;- x[N] * beta + u[random[N]];\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt;      y ~ normal(mu, sigma);\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt;    }\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt;    &quot;&quot;&quot;\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; Surprisingly to me, the code actually compiles. But I get an error:\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; Informational Message: The current Metropolis proposal is about to be rejected because of the following issue:\r<br>&gt; Exception thrown at line 24: stan::math::lognormal_log: Location parameter[1] is nan, but must be finite!\r<br>&gt; If this warning occurs sporadically, such as for highly constrained variable types like covariance matrices, then the sampler is fine,\r<br>&gt; but if this warning occurs often then your model may be either severely ill-conditioned or misspecified.\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; Rejecting initial value:\r<br>&gt;   Error evaluating the log probability at the initial value.\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; Initialization between (-2, 2) failed after 100 attempts. \r<br>&gt;  Try specifying initial values, reducing ranges of constrained values, or reparameterizing the model.\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; Using lme in R, I know this model has a solution. This seems it could very easily be something to do with my constraints or priors. What is going on with this model? Am I even in the ballpark?\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; \r<br>"
